#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
send2kodi - send URLs, local files and folders to Kodi.

  send2kodi https://youtu.be/jNQXAC9IVRw     # YouTube, or anything yt-dlp knows
  send2kodi https://host/clip.mp4            # a link to a media file or stream
  send2kodi ~/Videos/movie.mkv               # a file on this machine
  send2kodi ~/Videos/Some.Show/              # a folder, episodes in order
  send2kodi ~/Music/album/                   # songs play in the music player
  send2kodi ~/Pictures/*.jpg                 # pictures, as a slideshow
  send2kodi -s 1:02:30 ~/Videos/movie.mkv    # start at 1h02m30s
  send2kodi --sub subs.srt URL_OR_FILE       # with these subtitles
  send2kodi -q URL_OR_FILE ...               # append to Kodi's queue
  send2kodi -q -C                            # queue what's in the clipboard
  ls ~/Videos/*.mkv | send2kodi -            # read them from stdin
  send2kodi --bg ~/Videos/movie.mkv          # serve in the background
  send2kodi --stop | --pause

Everything goes through Kodi's JSON-RPC API; nothing has to be installed on
this machine beyond Python 3.

Links to media files and streams (.mp4, .mp3, .m3u8, ...) are played by
Kodi itself. YouTube videos are handed to the YouTube add-on
(plugin.video.youtube), which starts fastest, and at the time in the link
(?t=...). Other URLs, and YouTube when that add-on is missing or
YOUTUBE=ytdlp, go through the SendToKodi add-on (plugin.video.sendtokodi),
which resolves them with yt-dlp on the Kodi side.

Pictures (files, or image URLs) go to Kodi's picture viewer instead of the
video player, and stay on screen until you leave it; several make a
slideshow. Songs go to the music player, unless there are videos too.
Pictures can't be mixed with videos or songs in one run. A folder sends the
videos and songs in it and in its subfolders, or its pictures if it has
neither, in natural order (2 before 10).

Local files are served by a small HTTP server that exposes only the files
given on the command line, and the subtitles next to videos (Kodi finds
movie.srt, movie.en.srt, ... as it does next to its own files), under random
unguessable paths, answers only the Kodi box, and exits once Kodi has played
them or they leave the queue (or on Ctrl-C). With --bg it detaches once
playback has started, freeing the terminal.

Settings come from, in increasing priority: built-in defaults, the config
file (~/.config/send2kodi/config), the environment, and command-line flags.

https://github.com/hugoocoto/send2kodi
"""

